WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.471 --> 00:00:11.517
Tom Lindqvist: Hey Dale, Becca, like, Ryan. Thanks for making time this afternoon. How's the week going out there?

2
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:24.128
Dale Henderson: Pretty good. Busy. Harvest is in full swing, so half the office is either talking about beans or trying to get out of the office to go look at beans.

3
00:00:25.296 --> 00:00:34.782
Becca Lund: My brother started combining Tuesday. He sent me a picture at like six in the morning. I don't know what he wanted me to do about it.

4
00:00:35.252 --> 00:00:41.413
Ryan Fischer: Yeah, my in-laws farm over by Hartford, so we're on standby for babysitting while they run.

5
00:00:42.778 --> 00:00:54.812
Tom Lindqvist: That's the season. Okay, I've got us down for the quarterly review. I pulled the numbers for July through September. Want me to just walk through what I'm seeing, and you all jump in whenever?

6
00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.265
Dale Henderson: Yeah, go ahead.

7
00:00:56.831 --> 00:01:05.159
Tom Lindqvist: So call volume across tracking numbers was up about eleven percent quarter over quarter, which tracks with the new order flow going live in September.

8
00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:11.822
Tom Lindqvist: Google Ads drove most of that. The one thing that stands out to me is the last week of September.

9
00:01:12.651 --> 00:01:13.451
Becca Lund: The outage.

10
00:01:14.059 --> 00:01:19.679
Tom Lindqvist: The outage. September twenty-ninth, right? I saw the spike. What did that look like from your side?

11
00:01:20.643 --> 00:01:30.694
Becca Lund: So that that was a fiber cut over by the state line, took down a chunk of the network for most of the day.

12
00:01:30.694 --> 00:01:39.907
Becca Lund: Our care queue normally runs maybe sixty, seventy calls a day. That day it was, I don't know, north of two thousand.

13
00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.454
Ryan Fischer: It was a lot. Our status page got hammered too.

14
00:01:45.177 --> 00:01:56.787
Becca Lund: During the outage on the twenty-ninth the voice agent took thousands of calls, which was great, but every one it passed to my team came over with no notes.

15
00:01:57.223 --> 00:02:07.820
Tom Lindqvist: No notes. So the caller talks to the agent, the agent decides it needs a person, transfers, and the rep picks up with nothing on the screen?

16
00:02:08.505 --> 00:02:22.900
Becca Lund: Nothing. No name, no account, no idea what they already said. So my reps are starting over from scratch with somebody who's already been on hold for ten minutes and is not happy.

17
00:02:24.364 --> 00:02:29.169
Tom Lindqvist: How many of those transfers are we talking about that day?

18
00:02:29.856 --> 00:02:39.088
Becca Lund: I'd have to pull it, but but a few hundred at least. The ones that stayed with the agent were fine, honestly.

19
00:02:39.088 --> 00:02:41.606
Becca Lund: It was the handoffs that hurt.

20
00:02:42.002 --> 00:02:54.238
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. Let me write that down and take it back to the team. Can you get me one or two call IDs from that day so I can hand over actual examples?

21
00:02:54.985 --> 00:02:57.704
Becca Lund: Yeah, I can pull a couple this afternoon.

22
00:02:58.519 --> 00:03:08.662
Tom Lindqvist: That's perfect. And you know, just so I'm clear, this is specifically the transfer into your contact center, not the agent's own calls.

23
00:03:09.018 --> 00:03:13.230
Becca Lund: Right. The agent itself did its job. It's the handoff.

24
00:03:13.556 --> 00:03:17.488
Tom Lindqvist: Got it. Okay, Ryan, you wanted to talk about the order flow.

25
00:03:17.818 --> 00:03:25.221
Ryan Fischer: Yeah. So we rebuilt the whole thing as a single-page app, React, launched the second week of September. Generally it's been good.

26
00:03:25.221 --> 00:03:29.259
Ryan Fischer: Faster, fewer drop-offs. But we noticed something in the tracking pretty quick.

27
00:03:29.573 --> 00:03:30.373
Tom Lindqvist: What's that?

28
00:03:31.347 --> 00:03:38.631
Ryan Fischer: the number swap stopped working on our order pages after we moved to the new React app

29
00:03:39.768 --> 00:03:41.092
Tom Lindqvist: The dynamic number insertion.

30
00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:51.650
Ryan Fischer: Yeah. On the marketing site it's fine, numbers swap like they always did. But once you're inside the order flow, it's the static number.

31
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:57.583
Ryan Fischer: So any call from that page attributes to the wrong thing, or to nothing.

32
00:03:58.313 --> 00:04:07.073
Dale Henderson: Which matters because that's exactly where people call from. They get three steps in, something doesn't validate, they pick up the phone.

33
00:04:08.058 --> 00:04:10.784
Tom Lindqvist: How long has it been like that?

34
00:04:11.545 --> 00:04:27.595
Ryan Fischer: Since launch. We caught it maybe two weeks in. I assumed it was on our end, some lifecycle thing um, with the tag not firing on route changes, but I poked at it and couldn't pin it down.

35
00:04:28.374 --> 00:04:39.948
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. So one, the tag needs to fire on route change, not just initial page load. Two, there may be something about how the number pool is being read in that context.

36
00:04:39.948 --> 00:04:51.160
Tom Lindqvist: I I don't want to guess at it on the call. Can I get the URL pattern for one of those order pages, and roughly when the two weeks in was?

37
00:04:52.055 --> 00:04:56.813
Ryan Fischer: Yeah, I'll send you a staging link and a prod link. Both reproduce.

38
00:04:57.976 --> 00:05:03.583
Tom Lindqvist: Good. I'll get that in front of support today so it's not sitting over the weekend.

39
00:05:04.043 --> 00:05:04.843
Dale Henderson: Appreciate that.

40
00:05:06.269 --> 00:05:11.092
Tom Lindqvist: Dale, you had a a reporting thing on the list too.

41
00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:22.664
Dale Henderson: Yeah. So every Monday I pull the weekly source report, by campaign, by channel, and I paste it into a deck for our VP and the regional managers.

42
00:05:22.664 --> 00:05:31.088
Dale Henderson: It's not hard, it's just... every Monday, same thing. I mean, Download the CSV, clean it it up, um, paste it in.

43
00:05:31.741 --> 00:05:33.981
Tom Lindqvist: How long does that take you?

44
00:05:34.936 --> 00:05:44.632
Dale Henderson: Twenty minutes if nothing's weird. Forty if something's weird. And it's me doing it, which means if I'm traveling it doesn't happen.

45
00:05:45.703 --> 00:05:47.668
Tom Lindqvist: And where does it end up?

46
00:05:48.982 --> 00:05:59.122
Dale Henderson: Google Sheets. That's where the the deck pulls from. I'd love a way to get the weekly source report into Google Sheets without someone downloading it

47
00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:03.427
Tom Lindqvist: So a scheduled push, or a live connection, straight into a sheet.

48
00:06:03.662 --> 00:06:09.689
Dale Henderson: Either. I just don't want a um, human in the loop on a Tuesday morning.

49
00:06:10.535 --> 00:06:18.575
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. I'll take that one back too. I know we talked about it briefly in July, so let me see where it sits.

50
00:06:18.888 --> 00:06:19.688
Dale Henderson: Sure.

51
00:06:19.954 --> 00:06:25.915
Tom Lindqvist: Anything else on the list before I run through the the rest of the metrics?

52
00:06:27.284 --> 00:06:29.393
Becca Lund: No, I think that's the two.

53
00:06:30.702 --> 00:06:31.502
Ryan Fischer: Yeah.

54
00:06:32.877 --> 00:06:41.980
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. So the rest is pretty quiet. Call tracking attribution is holding steady, Google Ads conversions are flowing, Marketo's getting what it needs.

55
00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.750
Tom Lindqvist: Anything on your end feeling off there?

56
00:06:44.994 --> 00:06:46.336
Dale Henderson: No, that side's fine.

57
00:06:46.538 --> 00:06:50.881
Tom Lindqvist: Good. And the agent's handling routine outage calls the way you wanted?

58
00:06:52.071 --> 00:06:59.899
Becca Lund: Yeah, that part's working. People get the status message, so, they get the ETA, most of them hang up happy.

59
00:07:00.312 --> 00:07:02.718
Tom Lindqvist: Good. You're at sixty seats, still, right?

60
00:07:03.424 --> 00:07:08.207
Dale Henderson: Uh, Sixty-one as of last month. One new hire in care.

61
00:07:08.821 --> 00:07:17.245
Tom Lindqvist: I'll note that. Renewal's October of next year, so we've got plenty of runway, but if the seat count keeps creeping I'd rather know early.

62
00:07:18.692 --> 00:07:22.647
Dale Henderson: Probably one or two more before spring. Nothing dramatic.

63
00:07:23.641 --> 00:07:30.443
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. Let me get through the rest and then we can get you out of here.

64
00:07:31.231 --> 00:07:37.346
Ryan Fischer: Before you do, is the football game still on for tonight? I heard they moved it.

65
00:07:37.908 --> 00:07:41.811
Becca Lund: Moved to seven. Homecoming, so they wanted the lights.

66
00:07:42.704 --> 00:07:47.847
Ryan Fischer: Oh, that's why. My nephew's on the line, he's been talking about it all week.

67
00:07:48.591 --> 00:07:50.110
Tom Lindqvist: Who are they playing?

68
00:07:50.472 --> 00:07:54.946
Ryan Fischer: The team from over in Mitchell. Should be a good one. They're undefeated.

69
00:07:55.368 --> 00:07:56.536
Becca Lund: So are we.

70
00:07:57.031 --> 00:07:58.347
Ryan Fischer: We're four and three.

71
00:07:58.863 --> 00:07:59.663
Becca Lund: In conference.

72
00:08:00.736 --> 00:08:01.536
Ryan Fischer: Okay.

73
00:08:02.392 --> 00:08:12.707
Tom Lindqvist: I'll leave that one alone. Okay, so to wrap the numbers. Volume up eleven percent, uh, agent handled the outage traffic, attribution's clean outside the order flow issue.

74
00:08:12.707 --> 00:08:20.812
Tom Lindqvist: I've got three things: call IDs from Becca, the staging and prod links from Ryan, and the Google Sheets report for Dale.

75
00:08:21.523 --> 00:08:22.323
Dale Henderson: That's right.

76
00:08:23.084 --> 00:08:32.226
Tom Lindqvist: I'll send a follow-up today with those three items and who's on each. If the number swap thing comes back faster than I expect, I'll flag it.

77
00:08:33.231 --> 00:08:34.031
Ryan Fischer: Sounds good.

78
00:08:35.095 --> 00:08:39.774
Tom Lindqvist: Anything you want me to add or take off before I write it up?

79
00:08:41.165 --> 00:08:43.132
Becca Lund: No, that that covers it.

80
00:08:43.907 --> 00:08:45.463
Dale Henderson: Yeah, so, we're good.

81
00:08:46.608 --> 00:08:50.016
Tom Lindqvist: Great. Then go enjoy the game. Good luck tonight, Ryan.

82
00:08:50.315 --> 00:08:53.346
Ryan Fischer: Thanks. I'll be the one in the bad jacket.

83
00:08:53.698 --> 00:08:54.684
Becca Lund: You always are.

84
00:08:55.203 --> 00:08:57.760
Tom Lindqvist: Have a good weekend, everybody. Talk soon.

85
00:08:58.461 --> 00:09:00.140
Dale Henderson: You too, Tom. Thanks.
